{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In ensemble ML, base classifiers are typically trained to convergence on the same task; non-expert panelists are undertrained and rely on the checklist to substitute for missing expertise.","ML ensembles combine scores algorithmically; human panels may use social aggregation (majority vote, discussion) that introduces conformity bias not present in algorithmic ensembles.","Checklist items in a human panel require subjective perceptual judgment, whereas ML base classifiers produce deterministic outputs for fixed inputs."],"overturn_conditions":["If experts are shown to already subconsciously check the same features the checklist encodes, the checklist adds no new information and the panel is just a noisier version of the expert.","If non-expert panelists are found to be below chance on checklist items that require tacit knowledge, aggregation amplifies error rather than cancelling it."],"shared_structure":["A structured aggregation of weaker classifiers (non-experts + checklist) is compared against a single strong classifier (expert intuition) for a verifiable target.","The weaker classifiers must be individually above-chance and collectively diverse for aggregation to win.","The checklist functions as a shared feature template that constrains the weaker classifiers' decision space."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003","SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["As in ensembles, the panel's advantage should be largest in the 'medium difficulty' regime where the expert is fallible but non-experts can contribute signal.","As in ensembles, if the checklist (shared feature template) omits a critical feature, all base classifiers are correlated in their blindness and the panel fails together—no amount of aggregation recovers the missing feature."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For objectively verifiable tasks, a trained non-expert panel using an explicit checklist will be more reliable than a single expert using intuition, but only when the checklist encodes the causal features that determine the outcome and the panel's aggregate judgment suppresses individual noise.","counterconditions":["If the task outcome depends on holistic pattern recognition that trained experts possess but non-experts lack (e.g., radiological diagnosis), a checklist of surface features will be incomplete and the panel will underperform.","If the expert's intuition is itself well-calibrated and operates on the same diagnostic features the checklist encodes, the panel's extra noise may exceed its benefit.","If panel members' judgments are not independent—e.g., they discuss before scoring—the error-cancellation mechanism fails and the panel may merely amplify a shared checklist misreading."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists force decomposition of a complex judgment into independently checkable sub-claims. When each item maps to an objectively verifiable sub-condition (e.g., 'Does the specimen show feature X?'), the panel converts an opaque holistic judgment into a structured evidence-accumulation process. Aggregation across non-experts then cancels idiosyncratic perceptual errors (wisdom of crowds), while the checklist cancels systematic omission errors that plague expert intuition. The net reliability gain depends on the checklist's coverage of the outcome-determining feature space.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Polanyi's framing concerns whether tacit knowledge can be articulated at all; here the checklist is given and the question is empirical reliability, not philosophical commensurability.","Here we can measure outcomes objectively, so the dispute is resolvable by data rather than by conceptual analysis.","The panel adds an aggregation layer not present in the standard tacit-knowledge framing."],"overturn_conditions":["If panelists using the checklist are shown to match expert performance specifically on atypical, configural cases, the tacit-knowledge bottleneck is empirically refuted for this task.","If the checklist is shown to encode configural rules (e.g., 'If feature A is present and feature B is absent, escalate'), it has articulated the tacit knowledge, contradicting the claim that it cannot be encoded."],"shared_structure":["A mechanistic procedure (checklist) is compared to a holistic process (intuition) for a task where the outcome is objectively scorable.","The question is whether the mechanistic procedure captures the information content of the holistic process.","Tacit knowledge that is not articulated poses a challenge to the mechanistic procedure's completeness."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_002"],"transfer_claims":["As in the tacit-knowledge argument, the checklist's reliability on surface features may mask an inability to handle the deep features that determine outcomes in edge cases.","The evidence that would distinguish genuine expertise from mere checklist compliance is the same: performance on novel, atypical cases where surface and deep features diverge."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The non-expert panel will NOT be more reliable than the expert for objectively verifiable tasks, because checklists cannot encode the tacit feature-detection that makes expert intuition accurate; the panel's checklist-compliance merely mimics expertise on easy cases and collapses on the hard cases that determine overall reliability.","counterconditions":["If the task is genuinely decomposable into independent features with no configural interactions (e.g., 'Does the document contain a date in field 3?'), the checklist will match or beat the expert, and the panel's aggregation will reduce noise.","If the expert is poorly calibrated due to overconfidence or affective bias, the panel's mechanical procedure may outperform even on configural tasks.","If the checklist is adaptive—branching based on earlier answers in a way that captures configural dependencies—the tacit-knowledge objection is weakened."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition, in domains with verifiable outcomes, is typically the product of deliberate practice generating rapid pattern matching to diagnostic constellations. A checklist decomposes the judgment into isolated features, but the diagnostic value of a feature often depends on its context (configural processing). Non-experts applying the checklist item-by-item lose configural information. Therefore the panel's apparent reliability on items the checklist happens to cover is not a genuine competence transfer; it is a local heuristic that fails when features appear in atypical configurations—which are precisely the cases where expert intuition excels.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The reliability gain is genuine rather than mere compliance if and only if the panel's item-level judgments predict outcomes independently of the checklist's global answer key, and the panel shows transfer to reordered or rephrased checklists that the panelists have not seen.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is so detailed that it functions as a complete decision tree, independent item-level signal is unnecessary—the checklist itself is the expertise, and mere compliance is sufficient for reliability.","If reordering the checklist introduces confusion that degrades performance even for understanding panelists (e.g., because earlier items provide context for later items), the transfer test is invalid.","If the task is so simple that both understanding and compliance yield ceiling-level accuracy, the transfer test cannot distinguish them."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Mere checklist compliance that tracks the right answer means panelists are following the checklist's algorithm mechanically without understanding the items. In that case, their item-level responses are fully explained by the checklist's content and yield no independent predictive power. Genuine reliability gain requires that panelists have learned, through training, to recognize the features the checklist points to—so their responses carry signal beyond the checklist structure. Transfer to reordered or rephrased checklists is a direct test: if panelists rely on understanding rather than positional compliance, reordering items should not degrade their accuracy; if they rely on rote sequence, it will.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Here the judgment system is a human panel, not a probabilistic model; human miscalibration has psychological causes (overconfidence, anchoring) not present in ML.","The checklist introduces an external scaffold that may constrain human probability reporting in ways ML models are not constrained.","Calibration is evaluated within a single evaluation, not across many datasets as in ML benchmarking."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel is poorly calibrated but still more accurate than the expert (because the expert is also miscalibrated), calibration is not the distinguishing evidence.","If the expert is well-calibrated but the panel is not, and the panel is still more accurate, then calibration does not distinguish genuine gain from compliance—it may be that compliance is the better strategy."],"shared_structure":["A judgment system is evaluated by the correspondence between its stated confidence and observed outcome frequency.","Miscalibration indicates a disconnect between the judgment process and the true generating process.","Binary or coarse-grained outputs can mask miscalibration by collapsing the probability space."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["As in ML calibration, a panel that is overconfident on hard cases is not genuinely reliable even if its accuracy looks acceptable, because its errors are predictable rather than random.","A panel whose calibration holds under distribution shift (harder cases) has learned the features, not merely the checklist form."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The evidence that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere compliance is the panel's calibration curve: a genuine gain produces well-calibrated probability assessments across difficulty levels, while mere compliance produces overconfidence on hard cases and underconfidence on easy cases because the checklist's binary items do not map to graded confidence.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is designed to produce calibrated outputs (e.g., by weighting items by their diagnosticity), even mere compliance could yield good calibration.","If the task has binary outcomes and no meaningful intermediate probabilities, calibration analysis is uninformative.","If panelists report confidence without understanding, their confidence may be uninformative even if they understand the features—so calibration alone may not distinguish comprehension from well-calibrated guessing."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Trained non-experts who understand the features can report graded confidence (e.g., 'I'm 70% sure this item is present'), which aggregates into a calibrated probability. Mere compliance yields binary check-marks that, when summed, produce a pseudo-probability disconnected from true posterior probability. On hard cases where the checklist items are ambiguous, a compliant panelist defaults to 'present' or 'absent' based on a heuristic, generating systematic miscalibration. Thus a calibration curve (predicted probability vs. observed frequency) reveals whether the panel is tracking truth or tracking the form of the checklist.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The most discriminating evidence within a single evaluation is a mediation analysis: decompose the panel's aggregate score into checklist-driven variance and item-understanding-driven variance. A genuine reliability gain requires that the understanding-mediated path predicts outcomes independently of the checklist-driven path; mere compliance means the understanding path is near-zero and all predictive power flows through the checklist.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is perfectly diagnostic (all items perfectly predict outcome), there is no residual variance for understanding to explain, and the mediation test is uninformative.","If understanding operates entirely through the checklist—i.e., understanding helps panelists apply the checklist correctly but produces no independent signal—the mediation test will show zero understanding path even though understanding is causally important.","If the evaluation sample is too small, the understanding coefficient will be estimated with high variance and the test will lack power."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Within one evaluation, we can measure (a) each panelist's raw item responses, (b) the checklist's keyed answers, and (c) the outcome. If we model outcome ~ checklist_score + understanding_residual, where understanding_residual is the part of the panelist's judgment not explained by the checklist key, then a significant coefficient on understanding_residual indicates the panelist brings independent feature-recognition ability. If the coefficient is near zero, the panelist is merely complying with the checklist and any reliability gain is reducible to the checklist's content, not the panel's competence. This is a causal mediation test: the checklist is the mediator, and the panelist's training is the treatment.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35}],"simulation_proposals":[]}